    "problem_statement": "GlobalProfiler decorator logic inverted causing functions to be profiled when disabled\n\n#### Description\n\nThe GlobalProfiler's `__call__` method has inverted logic that causes functions to be profiled when profiling is disabled and not profiled when profiling is enabled.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom line_profiler import profile\n\n# Test 1: Default behavior (should not profile)\n@profile\ndef func1():\n    return 42\n\nfunc1()  # This gets profiled when it shouldn't\n\n# Test 2: Explicitly enable profiling\nprofile.enable()\n\n@profile  \ndef func2():\n    return 42\n\nfunc2()  # This doesn't get profiled when it should\n```\n\nThe issue manifests when using explicit profiling where:\n- Functions decorated with `@profile` get profiled even when profiling is disabled by default\n- Functions decorated with `@profile` don't get profiled when profiling is explicitly enabled\n\nThis breaks the expected behavior where profiling should only occur when explicitly requested via environment variables, command line flags, or programmatic enabling.",
